                    <!-- Part 8: Constructors in Inheritance -->
                    <section class="mb-5" data-aos="fade-up">
                        <div class="card border-0 shadow-lg" style="border-radius: 20px; overflow: hidden;">
                            <div class="card-header" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #f093fb 0%, #f5576c 100%); color: white; border: none;">
                                <h3 class="mb-0"><i class="fas fa-8 me-2"></i>Part 8: Constructors in Inheritance</h3>
                            </div>
                            <div class="card-body p-4">
                                <p class="fs-5">Constructors in inheritance follow a <strong>specific order</strong> - parents always come first!</p>

                                <div class="row">
                                    <div class="col-lg-6">
                                        <div class="card bg-light h-100">
                                            <div class="card-body">
                                                <h5><i class="fas fa-code text-primary me-2"></i>Constructor Execution Order</h5>
                                                <div class="code-block small">
                                                    <pre><code class="language-csharp"><span class="keyword">public</span> <span class="keyword">class</span> <span class="class">Grandparent</span>
{
    <span class="keyword">public</span> <span class="function">Grandparent</span>() 
    { 
        Console.WriteLine(<span class="string">"1. Grandparent"</span>); 
    }
}

<span class="keyword">public</span> <span class="keyword">class</span> <span class="class">Parent</span> : <span class="class">Grandparent</span>
{
    <span class="keyword">public</span> <span class="function">Parent</span>() 
    { 
        Console.WriteLine(<span class="string">"2. Parent"</span>); 
    }
}

<span class="keyword">public</span> <span class="keyword">class</span> <span class="class">Child</span> : <span class="class">Parent</span>
{
    <span class="keyword">public</span> <span class="function">Child</span>() 
    { 
        Console.WriteLine(<span class="string">"3. Child"</span>); 
    }
}</code></pre>
                                                </div>
                                            </div>
                                        </div>
                                    </div>
                                    <div class="col-lg-6">
                                        <div class="card bg-light h-100">
                                            <div class="card-body">
                                                <h5><i class="fas fa-play text-success me-2"></i>When You Create a Child</h5>
                                                <div class="code-block small">
                                                    <pre><code class="language-csharp"><span class="class">Child</span> c = <span class="keyword">new</span> <span class="class">Child</span>();

<span class="comment">// Output:</span>
<span class="comment">// 1. Grandparent  ← First!</span>
<span class="comment">// 2. Parent       ← Second!</span>
<span class="comment">// 3. Child        ← Last!</span></code></pre>
                                                </div>
                                                <div class="alert alert-success mt-2">
                                                    <i class="fas fa-lightbulb me-2"></i>
                                                    <strong>Why this order?</strong> The parent must be fully built before the child can use it.
                                                    Like building a house: Foundation → Walls → Roof
                                                </div>
                                            </div>
                                        </div>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
